From 99033dea8e493b30a1ac5544355edb86eae9efb7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mpenelange <191051681+mpenelange@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 21:58:07 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 12/18] Serialize flow-table control paths; bound flow export by the live table

The flow export (/proc/cavium/ipv4/flows) walked a fixed 8192 buckets
regardless of ipv4_cache_size. After shrinking the cache, one read ran
past the vmalloc'ed table and took bucket "spinlocks" in whatever lay
beyond; on the ER-4 that hard-locked a core until the NMI watchdog reset
the chip. After growing it, flows above bucket 8191 were never exported,
and with offload disabled the table pointer is NULL.

Add cvm_ipfwd_table_mutex and take it in every process-context user that
frees, replaces, resizes or walks a table: cache_size and fwd writes,
/proc flush and per-entry clear, cache dumps, the stamp-wrap wipe, module
exit, and the export (held from seq start to stop). The export now uses
ipv4_cache_size and checks the table exists.

Also:
- export_seq_next rewound *pos to 0 at the end, so a read never hit EOF
  and cat streamed the table forever
- cache_size writes enforce the documented maximum (32768)
- IPv6 disable freed the table before clearing the fwd flag, so the fast
  path could still be using it; stop the fast path first, as IPv4 does

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
